{"id":"ORTH-CONTRACT-007","category":"accepted_variant","forms":["buu","baa uu","ayuu","ayaa uu"],"status":"context_required","sources":["SLS resources/qoraal/02-eray-kooban-hadalka.md","Qaamuuska Af-Soomaaliga 2012 focus-particle examples","University of Gothenburg Somali grammar/workbook contraction tables"],"note":"Masculine-singular focus contractions buu (baa + uu) and ayuu (ayaa + uu) are accepted written Somali alongside expanded analyses. Preserve the contraction; do not force expansion."}
{"id":"ORTH-CONTRACT-008","category":"accepted_variant","forms":["bay","baa ay","ayay","ayaa ay"],"status":"context_required","sources":["SLS resources/qoraal/02-eray-kooban-hadalka.md","Qaamuuska Af-Soomaaliga 2012 focus-particle examples","University of Gothenburg Somali grammar/workbook contraction tables"],"note":"Focus contractions bay (baa + ay) and ayay (ayaa + ay) are accepted written Somali. The short subject pronoun ay can be feminine singular or third-person plural, so grammatical context resolves person; do not force expansion."}
{"id":"ORTH-CONTRACT-009","category":"accepted_variant","forms":["way","waa ay"],"status":"context_required","sources":["SLS resources/qoraal/02-eray-kooban-hadalka.md","project native review — Jigjiga Somali"],"note":"SLS records the expansion waa ay. Project native review confirms way as natural written Somali in ordinary statements. Preserve both as evidence; do not flag or automatically rewrite either form."}
